From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: a733: add the thermal sensor, five zones, and CPU throttling

Wires the THS the previous patches enable, and gives the board actual thermal
protection rather than a readable number.

The node: base and size from the memory map of the A733 user manual (UM V0.92),
IRQ INTID 71 = GIC SPI 39 (UM interrupt list); the two
clocks and the reset from `clk-sunxi-ng-a733-add-ths-and-gpadc-clocks`. No nvmem-cells and no allwinner,sram, for the
reasons `thermal-sun8i-add-a733-support` records.

Every sensor the part has gets a zone. A server image that watches one corner
of the die and not the others is not protection, and the four non-CPU sensors
cost nothing to read.

The two CPU sensor indices are measured, and they contradict the UM. This is the
only value in this patch whose source is silicon alone, and it was the only one
that went in unquestioned -- because it was, for once, documented.

The UM states the mapping outright (A733 user manual, 7.1.1): the die has five
thermal sensors, sensor0 in the CPUB, sensor1 in the DDR, sensor2 in the NPU,
sensor3 in the CPUL and sensor4 in the GPU.
The datasheet says only that
five sensors exist and where (datasheet V0.92 and V0.93, "A76 core, A56 core, GPU,
NPU and DDR"), without indices. The UM's own environment diagram
draws both CPU sensors as one block, "Tsensor0、3", and so cannot arbitrate. Every
earlier revision of this patch and of `thermal-sun8i-add-a733-support`'s chip comment
took that sentence at face value.

A per-cluster stimulus refutes it for the two CPU entries. On the 8 GiB board, 2026-08-15,
test image, phases idle 120 s / A76-only 900 s / settle 180 s / A55-only
300 s: loading only cpu6+cpu7 raised ths 3 by +34.1 K against ths 0 +30.9 K;
loading only cpu0..cpu5 reversed it, ths 0 +15.6 K against ths 3 +14.2 K. The
ordering flips with the stimulus. Because each pair is a comparison between two
sensors inside one run, no baseline error, ambient drift, differing run length or
differing absolute power can produce the reversal -- only proximity to the heat
source can. ths 3 sits at the A76 and ths 0 at the A55, the other way round from
the UM. Re-reducing the same series over end-of-phase means rather than the
recorded window shifts each absolute rise by 1-2 K and leaves the gaps at 3.0 K
and 1.3 K, so the flip is a property of the data and not of the reduction.

It had already been measured once, and dismissed. On 2026-08-14 the same
stimulus ran for 120 s per cluster
and produced the same reversal: under A55-only THS0 60.82 C against THS3 58.96 C
(THS3-THS0 = -1.86 K), under A76-only THS0 74.81 C against THS3 78.17 C
(THS3-THS0 = +3.36 K), with the two sensors within 0.9 K of each other at every
idle point in between (+0.06, +0.87, +0.00 K). That is a 5.2 K stimulus-driven
swing in the difference, on a different day, a different image and a different
run length than the 08-15 series -- an independent replication, not a re-reading
of the same data.

It was set aside as inconclusive because the separations "1.9 K and 3.4 K" were
judged to be "inside the sensors' own +-3 C error band".
That reasoning does not hold, and it
is worth writing down because it is an easy mistake to repeat. The +-3 C figure
(UM 7.1.2) is an ABSOLUTE accuracy bound: how far one sensor's
reported temperature may sit from the true temperature. The measurement here is a
DIFFERENCE between two sensors compared across two stimuli, so any fixed
per-sensor offset -- which is exactly what an accuracy bound describes -- cancels
identically. An accuracy band cannot manufacture a sign reversal that tracks
which cluster is loaded, and the idle points show the pair returning to within
0.9 K each time. The one thing the +-3 C rating does bear on, absolute die
temperature, is not what this claim rests on.

So the sensors are swapped and the cooling maps are left alone: cpub-thermal
takes <&ths 3>, cpul-thermal takes <&ths 0>, and each zone keeps the cluster its
name promises. Swapping the cooling devices instead would have produced exactly
the same throttling behaviour while leaving a zone called "cpub" reporting the
little cluster's temperature -- correct behaviour resting on a false name, and
the version that a later reader, or a glance at the vendor DT, "fixes" back.

What the crossed mapping cost, and why it passed its own HW proof: under an
all-core load it is invisible. Both sensors rise together, both zones cross their
trip, both clusters throttle, and every acceptance run in this tree is an all-core
run -- this patch's own proof included. It bites only on a single-cluster load, where
the A76's sensor drove the A55's cpufreq policy and vice versa: the governor
throttled the cores that were not producing the heat and left the cores that were
at full clock. The lesson is the test, not the value: a sensor-to-block mapping is
only exercised by a stimulus that can tell the zones apart.

⚠ UNPROVEN, and deliberately not claimed here: why the UM disagrees. The stimulus
establishes which sensor is near which cluster; it cannot say whether Allwinner
transposed the sensors, the block names, or only that table. Nor is the shared
scale's absolute accuracy involved -- the argument is differential and survives
any common offset. The UM sentence is only flagged here, not rewritten:
the measurements settle which index this DT uses, not what Allwinner
got wrong.

Throttling is wired for both clusters and it is the only cooling this tree can
offer today. cpufreq-dt owns the CPU-little and CPU-big policies (`arm64-dts-a733-cpu-clocks-from-cpu-ccu` and `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-opp-tables-and-supply`), so
#cooling-cells goes on cpu@0 and cpu@600 -- the two policy owners, for the same
reason cpu-supply sits only there -- and each CPU zone maps its passive trip
onto its own cluster. DDR, NPU and GPU have no devfreq in this tree, so they
carry a critical trip and no cooling map: an emergency shutdown is real
protection, while an unwired passive trip would only look like it.

Trip temperatures now rest on the datasheet and on measurement, where an
earlier revision of this patch had to guess. Two things changed on 2026-08-14.

The limit is sourced. A733 Datasheet ch. 6 Table 6-2 gives a recommended
operating junction temperature of 115 C and an absolute maximum of 125 C, and
5.1.3 defines them apart: the first is continuous operation over the designed
lifetime, the second is where damage occurs. Identical in V0.92 and V0.93, whose
thermal chapters were diffed line by line. Design against 115, never 125.

The scale is no longer provisional in the way this patch used to claim. `thermal-sun8i-add-a733-support`'s
two-segment mapping was compared against the vendor driver's own input/output
relation -- its THS_DATA register paired with the temperature it reports for the
same sensor -- on three kernels including the untouched stock vendor ROM. The
relation recovered from that data by regression is slope -62.000 mC/LSB at
intercept 174964 below the breakpoint and -59.000 at 167265 above it: `thermal-sun8i-add-a733-support`'s own
two lines and its own knee, to the millidegree, 393/420 and 298/313 samples
exact. So the 4.6 C disagreement between the segments is the vendor's fit, not an
artefact of ours, and reproducing it is the correct behaviour rather than a
liability. What remains unproven is the absolute accuracy of that shared scale,
for which a traceable thermometer would be needed.

That leaves the sensor's own rating as the margin to carry: +-3 C from 60 to
125 C. Critical therefore moves 95 -> 110 C, matching what Allwinner ships and
what our own 6.6 track runs, and a reading of 110 C means at most 113 C true --
still under the 115 C the datasheet allows continuously.

Passive moves 80 -> 90 C, chosen fanless-first on purpose. Assume no board
running this kernel has a fan -- most will not -- and pick the value that is
right for that case. A fan then only keeps the part further away from the trip;
it is a bonus, not a precondition, and no trip needs to be re-tuned when one
exists.

80 C was not unsafe, it was leaving performance on the table for no gain. Measured
on the board with passive at 80 C: it settles at 82-83 C with the cooling states
at 4 of 10 and 3 of 13, i.e. cpu0 held to 1404 MHz and cpu6 to 1794 MHz. At 90 C
it settles near 92-93 C and gives those steps back, still 23 C inside the
datasheet's 115 C continuous limit, with the sensor's own +-3 C inside that
margin.

⚠ Read those clock numbers with their condition attached: the boards here have
their fans wired straight to +5 V and ground, bypassing the PWM connector, so a
fan was running at full speed throughout that measurement. The settling
temperature is set by the trip and is unaffected -- step_wise holds the die a few
degrees above it either way -- but the FREQUENCIES are the best case. A board with
no airflow at all reaches the same 82-83 C with more throttling and lower clocks.
The trip choice does not change; the performance it buys does, and nobody should
quote 1404/1794 MHz as a fanless figure. A genuinely fanless number has not been
measured, because no board here is genuinely fanless.

Verified in the decompiled dtb: five zones, #cooling-cells = 2 on both policy
CPUs, and the two cooling-device phandles resolving to cpu@0 and cpu@600.

HW proof pending: zones appear under /sys/class/thermal with plausible idle
readings, and under an eight-core load the temperature rises and
cooling_device*/cur_state leaves 0.

The two CPU zones carry labels (`cpul_thermal:`, `cpub_thermal:`) even though
nothing in this patch references them. That is deliberate: a board that adds an
active stage has to reach these zones with `&cpub_thermal { ... }`, and the label
belongs to the patch that defines the node rather than to whichever patch happens
to need it first. Without that, the board fan patch had to add the labels itself,
which silently made the idle-injection patch depend on the fan patch -- two
changes with nothing to do with each other, coupled through one context line, and
the coupling only showed up when the fan patch was held back for lacking hardware
proof.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
@@ -21,6 +21,7 @@
 #include <dt-bindings/clock/sun60i-a733-rtc-ccu.h>
 #include <dt-bindings/clock/sun60i-a733-serdes-ccu.h>
 #include <dt-bindings/gpio/gpio.h>
+#include <dt-bindings/thermal/thermal.h>
 #include <dt-bindings/reset/sun60i-a733-ccu.h>
 #include <dt-bindings/reset/sun60i-a733-r-ccu.h>
 #include <dt-bindings/reset/sun60i-a733-serdes-ccu.h>
@@ -1441,6 +1442,43 @@
 		 * unstable this is the first knob to turn.
 		 *
 		 */
+		/*
+		 * Thermal sensor controller. Base and size from the memory map of the A733 user manual
+		 * (UM V0.92); IRQ INTID 71 = GIC SPI 39 from
+		 * the interrupt list of the A733 user manual.
+		 *
+		 * Five sensors. The UM (7.1.1) assigns them
+		 * 0 = CPUB, 1 = DDR, 2 = NPU, 3 = CPUL, 4 = GPU. Three of those five
+		 * are used as written. The TWO CPU ENTRIES ARE MEASURED THE OTHER WAY
+		 * ROUND and this DT follows the measurement, not the UM: ths 0 is at
+		 * the A55 and ths 3 at the A76. Stimulus, deltas, and why the reversal
+		 * cannot be an artefact: the thermal-zones comment at the end of this
+		 * file.
+		 * Do not "restore" these two indices from the UM or from a vendor DT.
+		 *
+		 * Two clocks. "bus" is the THS0 gate; "mod" is the 24 MHz conversion
+		 * clock, whose CCU reset default is OFF -- without it the ADC has no
+		 * clock at all and the driver's 2 us / 20 us timing constants are
+		 * meaningless. It must be the 24 MHz SYS_CLK24M net, never the 26 MHz
+		 * DCXO.
+		 *
+		 * No nvmem-cells: the driver does not implement the fuse-to-CDATA
+		 * transform (known from the vendor driver, not from the UM; see
+		 * `thermal-sun8i-add-a733-support`), and shipping on the 0x800 reset default costs
+		 * -0.74..+0.43 C measured across five sensors on two dies -- well
+		 * inside the part's own +-3 C.
+		 * No allwinner,sram either; that is an H616 erratum, not this part.
+		 */
+		ths: thermal-sensor@2522000 {
+			compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-ths";
+			reg = <0x0 0x02522000 0x0 0x400>;
+			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 39 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+			clocks = <&ccu CLK_BUS_THS>, <&ccu CLK_GPADC0_24M>;
+			clock-names = "bus", "mod";
+			resets = <&ccu RST_BUS_THS>;
+			#thermal-sensor-cells = <1>;
+		};
+
 		gmac0: ethernet@4500000 {
 			compatible = "allwinner,sun60iw2-gmac200",
 				     "snps,dwmac-5.30a";
@@ -2336,3 +2374,214 @@
 &cpu7 {
 	operating-points-v2 = <&cluster1_opp>;
 };
+
+
+/*
+ * Thermal zones. Every sensor the part has gets one, because a server image
+ * that watches one corner of the die and not the others is not protection.
+ *
+ * ⚠ THE TWO CPU SENSOR INDICES ARE MEASURED, AND THEY CONTRADICT THE UM. This
+ * is the one value in this patch whose only source is silicon, so it is spelled
+ * out here in full rather than cited.
+ *
+ * The UM is not silent here -- it is wrong here, which is worse, because a
+ * documented value does not get questioned. UM 7.1.1 states that the die
+ * carries five thermal sensors: sensor0 in the CPUB, sensor1 in the DDR,
+ * sensor2 in the NPU, sensor3 in the CPUL, sensor4 in the GPU. Its own
+ * environment diagram
+ * draws both CPU sensors as a
+ * single block, "Tsensor0、3", so it distinguishes nothing and cannot correct
+ * the sentence. Taken at face value that sentence put <&ths 0> on cpub-thermal
+ * and <&ths 3> on cpul-thermal.
+ *
+ * A per-cluster stimulus on the 8 GiB board, 2026-08-15, reverses it. Load ONLY cpu6+cpu7
+ * (A76) for 900 s: ths 3 rises +34.1 K, ths 0 +30.9 K. Then load ONLY cpu0..5
+ * (A55) for 300 s: ths 0 rises +15.6 K, ths 3 +14.2 K. The ordering flips with
+ * the stimulus. That is a comparison of two sensors inside one run, so no
+ * baseline error, ambient drift, run length or absolute power level can produce
+ * the reversal -- only proximity to the heat source can. ths 3 is therefore at
+ * the A76 and ths 0 at the A55. Re-reducing the same series over end-of-phase
+ * means instead of the recorded window moves each absolute rise by 1-2 K and
+ * leaves the gaps at 3.0 K and 1.3 K: the flip is in the data, not in how it was
+ * reduced.
+ *
+ * Replicated, and previously dismissed. The same stimulus on 2026-08-14
+ * (120 s per cluster, different
+ * image) gave the same reversal: THS3-THS0 = -1.86 K under A55-only and
+ * +3.36 K under A76-only, with the pair inside 0.9 K at every idle point
+ * between them -- a 5.2 K stimulus-driven swing in the difference. It was set
+ * aside as "inside the sensors' own +-3 C error band".
+ * That does not follow: +-3 C
+ * (UM 7.1.2) is an ABSOLUTE accuracy bound on one sensor
+ * against true temperature, and this is a DIFFERENCE between two sensors
+ * across two stimuli, in which any fixed per-sensor offset cancels. An
+ * accuracy band cannot produce a sign reversal that tracks which cluster is
+ * loaded.
+ *
+ * The fix swaps the SENSORS, not the cooling maps, so each zone keeps the
+ * cluster its name promises and cpub-thermal reads the sensor at the big
+ * cluster. Swapping the cooling devices instead would have produced identical
+ * throttling while leaving a zone called "cpub" reporting the little cluster --
+ * correct behaviour standing on a false name, which is the version a later
+ * reader "fixes" back.
+ *
+ * Why the crossed version survived its own HW proof, stated because it is the
+ * reusable lesson: under an all-core load it is INVISIBLE. Both sensors rise
+ * together, both zones cross their trip, both clusters throttle, and every
+ * acceptance run this tree has done is an all-core run. It bites only on a
+ * single-cluster load, where the A76's sensor drove the A55's cpufreq policy and
+ * vice versa -- the governor throttled cores that were not producing the heat
+ * and left the cores that were at full clock. A thermal mapping is only tested
+ * by a stimulus that can tell the two zones apart.
+ *
+ * ⚠ UNPROVEN, and deliberately not claimed: WHY the UM disagrees. The stimulus
+ * establishes which sensor sits near which cluster; it cannot say whether
+ * Allwinner transposed the sensors, the block names, or only that table. The UM
+ * sentence is only flagged here, not rewritten: the measurements settle
+ * which index this DT uses, not what Allwinner got wrong.
+ *
+ *
+ * ⚠ Trip temperatures come from the datasheet, not from a guess. A733 Datasheet
+ * Table 6-2: recommended operating junction temperature 115 C, absolute maximum
+ * 125 C; 5.1.3 defines the first as continuous operation over the designed
+ * lifetime and the second as the damage threshold. Design against 115.
+ * Critical 110 C leaves the sensor's own +-3 C rating inside that limit -- a
+ * reading of 110 is at most 113 true -- and is what both Allwinner and our 6.6
+ * track ship.
+ *
+ * Passive 90 C is chosen fanless-first: assume no board running this kernel has
+ * a fan, because most will not. Measured at 80 C the part settles at 82-83 C with
+ * cpu0 throttled to 1404 MHz and cpu6 to 1794 MHz; at 90 C it settles near
+ * 92-93 C and gives those steps back, still 23 C inside the datasheet's 115 C
+ * continuous limit. ⚠ Those clock figures were taken with a fan running flat out
+ * -- the boards here have theirs wired directly to +5 V, bypassing PWM -- so they
+ * are the best case. The settling temperature is set by the trip and does not
+ * change; a board with no airflow reaches it with more throttling. The vendor's 60 C works only because a fan
+ * runs from a 60 C active trip -- without one, step_wise parks at the bottom OPP
+ * (416 MHz on their table, 247 MHz on ours), which is a misconfiguration rather
+ * than caution.
+ *
+ * The scale itself was checked rather than trusted: `thermal-sun8i-add-a733-support`'s two segments were
+ * measured against the vendor driver's own register-to-reported-temperature
+ * relation on three kernels, including an untouched stock vendor ROM, and match
+ * it to the millidegree on both segments. The 4.6 C step between the segments is
+ * the vendor's, and reproducing it is correct. Absolute accuracy of that shared
+ * scale is still unproven and needs a traceable thermometer.
+ *
+ * ⚠ Throttling is still the only actuator between here and the critical trip.
+ * The 6.6 track has a second stage, idle injection at 100 C, and this tree gains
+ * it in `arm64-dts-a733-idle-injection-cooling`. Until that lands, the 20 C between 90 and 110 has nothing in it.
+ *
+ * Throttling is wired for both clusters and is the only cooling this tree can
+ * offer today: cpufreq-dt owns the CPU-big and CPU-little policies (`arm64-dts-a733-cpu-clocks-from-cpu-ccu` and `arm64-dts-a733-cpu-opp-tables-and-supply`),
+ * and there is no devfreq for DDR, NPU or GPU. Those three therefore carry a
+ * critical trip and no cooling map -- an emergency shutdown is real protection,
+ * an unwired passive trip would only look like it.
+ */
+&cpu0 {
+	#cooling-cells = <2>;
+};
+
+&cpu6 {
+	#cooling-cells = <2>;
+};
+
+/ {
+	thermal-zones {
+		cpul_thermal: cpul-thermal {
+			polling-delay-passive = <500>;
+			polling-delay = <1000>;
+			/* ths 0 = A55, measured; the UM says 3. See above. */
+			thermal-sensors = <&ths 0>;
+
+			trips {
+				cpul_passive: cpul-trip-0 {
+					temperature = <90000>;
+					type = "passive";
+					hysteresis = <2000>;
+				};
+				cpul_critical: cpul-trip-1 {
+					temperature = <110000>;
+					type = "critical";
+					hysteresis = <0>;
+				};
+			};
+
+			cooling-maps {
+				map0 {
+					trip = <&cpul_passive>;
+					cooling-device = <&cpu0 THERMAL_NO_LIMIT THERMAL_NO_LIMIT>;
+				};
+			};
+		};
+
+		cpub_thermal: cpub-thermal {
+			polling-delay-passive = <500>;
+			polling-delay = <1000>;
+			/* ths 3 = A76, measured; the UM says 0. See above. */
+			thermal-sensors = <&ths 3>;
+
+			trips {
+				cpub_passive: cpub-trip-0 {
+					temperature = <90000>;
+					type = "passive";
+					hysteresis = <2000>;
+				};
+				cpub_critical: cpub-trip-1 {
+					temperature = <110000>;
+					type = "critical";
+					hysteresis = <0>;
+				};
+			};
+
+			cooling-maps {
+				map0 {
+					trip = <&cpub_passive>;
+					cooling-device = <&cpu6 THERMAL_NO_LIMIT THERMAL_NO_LIMIT>;
+				};
+			};
+		};
+
+		ddr-thermal {
+			polling-delay-passive = <500>;
+			polling-delay = <1000>;
+			thermal-sensors = <&ths 1>;
+
+			trips {
+				ddr_critical: ddr-trip-0 {
+					temperature = <110000>;
+					type = "critical";
+					hysteresis = <0>;
+				};
+			};
+		};
+
+		npu-thermal {
+			polling-delay-passive = <500>;
+			polling-delay = <1000>;
+			thermal-sensors = <&ths 2>;
+
+			trips {
+				npu_critical: npu-trip-0 {
+					temperature = <110000>;
+					type = "critical";
+					hysteresis = <0>;
+				};
+			};
+		};
+
+		gpu-thermal {
+			polling-delay-passive = <500>;
+			polling-delay = <1000>;
+			thermal-sensors = <&ths 4>;
+
+			trips {
+				gpu_critical: gpu-trip-0 {
+					temperature = <110000>;
+					type = "critical";
+					hysteresis = <0>;
+				};
+			};
+		};
+	};
+};
